// Direct3D 11: the device, the pictures of windows, pipelines, and the images and buffers on the GPU.
//
// How it differs from the Vulkan backend (the shared code is the same):
// - There are no command buffers (a deferred context would not keep the order that the shared code counts on, see below), so the backend keeps a
//   tape of its own: the calls that Vulkan records (passes, draws, copies) are written down and done on the immediate context when the recording ends
//   (end_commands, submit_and_wait). The shared code writes to a mapped buffer after it recorded the draw that reads it, which is why this is not done at once.
// - Buffers that the CPU writes (memory::mapped and memory::mesh) have no persistent mapping in Direct3D 11. They are a copy in system memory (gpu_buffer::mapped points to it)
//   and, where a GPU reads them, a buffer of the device. The copy is compared with what was last sent when the tape binds the buffer, and what changed is sent with UpdateSubresource.
//   Buffers that are only copied to or from images (staging, readback) have no buffer of the device: the copies read and write the system memory.
// - Shaders are bytecode that fxc made from the HLSL of code_runtime/source/d3d11 (CMake), found by size and hash of the SPIR-V words (tools/generate_d3d11_table.py makes the table, with the
//   registers of every (set, binding), the constants and the push constants). Nothing is compiled at run time. The specialization constants of Vulkan are a constant buffer
//   (bb_constants) that every pipeline has its own immutable copy of, the push constants a small constant buffer that is updated before a draw.
// - Clip space: Vulkan has Y pointing down in clip space, Direct3D up. The vertex shaders negate Y (tools/translate_shaders.py, d3d11_vertex_fixups), so the viewport,
//   the winding of the pipelines and every picture stay what they are on Vulkan, and nothing here flips anything. Depth runs from 0 to 1 on both.
// - gl_InstanceIndex has the first instance in it on Vulkan, SV_InstanceID on Direct3D does not: the 3D vertex shader reads BBINSTANCE, a second vertex buffer of 0, 1, 2, ... that
//   advances once per instance and starts at the first instance of the draw (so the commands that the GPU makes keep working).
// - Direct3D 11 has no swizzle of a view, so a texture of blocks that the shared code asks to see with its channels changed (BC4 as grey, alpha forced to 1) shows them as they are.
// - Images are not in states: transition_image does nothing but show the picture of a window when it changes to present.
// - The Present of a window that has a thread of its own (the presenter) is done by that thread, under the lock of the context.
